 "productionNotes": "No figure in this car's history is settled, and the disagreement runs in two directions at once. On the American side, De Tomaso's own legacy page reports that Lincoln-Mercury claimed 6,091 Panteras had been brought to the United States by the end of 1974, and Conceptcarz's data record repeats 6,091 as the 1971-1974 production figure. Hagerty puts 5,674 sold by the end of 1974 and separately reports De Tomaso's own count as 5,286. Classic Motorsports gives a range of 5,233 to 6,091 in its feature and 5,244 in its buying guide. Wikipedia, Silodrome and Pantera Place round to around 5,500. De Tomaso Forum members working from NHTSA import records arrive at 5,262, excluding Canada. No source consulted reconciles these, and the spread between highest and lowest is over 800 cars. On the whole-run side, the total most often repeated is 7,260 cars built between 1971 and 1992; a forum reconstruction of the chassis sequence arrives at 7,082, and Hagerty says about 7,200. Record-keeping was poor, the chassis sequence was restarted in the 9000 range around 1980 and three coachbuilders bodied the car in succession, which is a plausible mechanism for the discrepancy but not a resolution of it. Variant counts are equally soft: the GT5 is given as fewer than 197, a maximum of 179, and up to 252 or 253 by registry reconstructions of chassis 9100-9374; the GT5-S as fewer than 183, about 190, 187 and 182 by four different sources. The 90 Si at 41 built, of which 38 were sold to customers, and the Group 4 at 14 cars are the only figures encountered here that more than one source states identically. Because the headline totals are contested at both the American and the global level, no productionTotal is asserted.",
